module contract;

// W47-CONDVAR-JOIN-STOP-PATHS (gateway rule 10q as amended, contract
// specification §C04.10 and §C06.1). The engine thread's start, stop and the
// shutdown joins of the threads it starts, as observed in the bodies.
//
// ## Why every declaration here awaits review
//
// What these functions do to a thread -- start it, hand it `ctx`, join it,
// ignore the join's result, return without joining -- is not a resource-v1
// custody fact, and v1 has no clause for it. So each declaration is
// **withheld** (`resource_review_pending`, §C06.1): it loads, it is pinned, it
// discharges nothing and accuses nothing, every call that would apply it is
// held under `awaiting-human-review` (a verdict resting only on such holds is
// `Pending`, never `Safe`), and no consumer applies the custody clauses below
// while the marker stands. The reason strings carry the observations; they
// are data for the reviewer, not assumptions of any analysis.
//
// ## Which observations are checked, and against what
//
// * **Checked against the body by the rule 10q census** (the lexical path
//   reader over the `90978ac6` export, `docs/reports/w47-condvar-join-stop-paths/`):
//   `flb_stop`'s branches (the `!ctx->config` return derived infeasible under
//   P-DEREF; the status branch's unchecked join; the checked join returned),
//   `do_start`'s two unchecked joins on its failure returns, the repeated
//   join a failed `flb_start` followed by `flb_stop` performs, and
//   `mk_utils_worker_spawn`'s joinable creation.
// * **Read, not derived by the census**: the shutdown joins
//   (`flb_input_thread_instance_exit`, `flb_output_thread_pool_destroy`,
//   `flb_downstream_worker_runtime_stop`). The census leaves every
//   descendant of the engine thread unresolved: the engine thread's path from
//   its entry to its exit is not read.
// * **Body identity**: `quirin-audit pin` records each body's identity at the
//   revisions it was reviewed against (`contracts.lock`); a changed body puts
//   the declaration out of force until it is reviewed again.
//
// ## Custody clauses, pending with the rest
//
// `ctx` and `ins` are not freed by these calls (`flb_stop` releases
// `ctx->config->cf_main`, a member, and `flb_output_thread_pool_destroy`
// releases `ins->tp`); `flb_downstream_worker_runtime_stop` frees
// `runtime` except on its self-join refusal; the results are scalars.
// `flb_start` hands `ctx` to the engine thread until it is joined, which
// `resource_borrowed` does not say: that clause is exactly what a reviewer
// must settle.
//
// W47-REVIEW-HOLD-CONCRETE-REASON: two markers end with
// `rewrite_before_acceptance` (contract specification C06.1), because their
// own reasons say the text as written is not what the body does:
// `flb_start` retains `ctx` until the engine thread is joined, which
// `resource_borrowed(ctx)` denies, and `flb_downstream_worker_runtime_stop`
// does not free `runtime` on its `-1` return, which
// `resource_released(runtime, nullable)` asserts on every return. The other
// three state custody the reasons do not contradict and stay plain. The
// category is published on every row holding the call and changes no verdict.
//
// Nothing here is a verified race, a verified defect or an `Unsafe` claim. A
// "candidate" names an upstream path whose confirmation needs a native
// reproduction.

// ## The strictly paired lifecycle (W47-FLB-LIB-LIFECYCLE and
// W47-FLB-LIB-STRICT-PAIRING, contract specification §C04.11, rule 10q as
// amended)
//
// `requires resource_lifecycle(ctx, start|stop|destroy)` states the library
// API as the user decided it (2026-09-29; this supersedes the earlier "a stop
// after the first has no effect"): `flb_stop` is permitted only after a
// successful `flb_start`, exactly once per successful start; after a failed
// `flb_start` the caller calls only `flb_destroy`; a second `flb_stop` is a
// caller violation; a start without a stop in between is not permitted, and
// a start after a stop is not derived from the bodies, so it is refused.
// `flb_destroy` is permitted from created, after a failed start, and after
// the stop. The clauses are **specifications to check, never facts**: L1 and
// L3-L6 are caller preconditions (they only refuse: a release a path
// violating one reaches never discharges); L2 and L7 describe the bodies and
// are applied only where every path the rule 10q census follows satisfies
// them. No per-unit route derives a context's lifecycle state, so there every
// call of these three functions is refused (`outside its declared lifecycle
// obligation`), behind the review hold that applies first.
//
// Checked at the 90978ac6 export, 046accf6 and 66910c10
// (docs/reports/w47-flb-lib-strict-pairing/): L2 (the first stop after a
// start that returned 0 joins the thread that start began, once) is satisfied
// by `flb_stop`'s body on every followed path (:1058 or :1077), so it is in
// force; L7 (a start that returns nonzero leaves no thread it began unjoined,
// the body fact behind L6's admission of a destroy after a failed start) is
// held: `do_start` joins the engine thread on both failure returns after the
// spawn (:984, :999), but the reader cannot tell a failed
// `mk_utils_worker_spawn` (:969-972) from a spawn, so one followed nonzero
// return leaves `flb_lib_worker` unjoined. What `flb_stop` does from a state
// the strict pair forbids is outside the contract: every feasible path joins
// `ctx->config->worker` (:1058 or :1077), which nothing clears, so a second
// stop and a stop after a failed start join a joined thread again and a stop
// of a never-started context, or after a start that failed before its spawn,
// joins an identifier no start produced; the native observation
// (docs/reports/w47-fb-double-stop-observation/) reproduced each. Those are
// reproductions of caller-contract violations, not library defects.

extern fn flb_start(ctx: ptr<mut, u8>) -> i32
contract {
    requires resource_contract_v1;
    requires resource_lifecycle(ctx, start);
    requires resource_review_pending("Observed at 90978ac6 (src/flb_lib.c), checked by the rule 10q census: do_start spawns the engine thread flb_lib_worker through mk_utils_worker_spawn, which creates it joinable (PTHREAD_CREATE_JOINABLE, lib/monkey/mk_core/mk_utils.c:316-318), and stores its identifier in ctx->config->worker (:973). On both failure returns after the spawn (:980-986, :994-1001) it joins the thread with the result ignored and sets ctx->status to FLB_LIB_ERROR. It returns 0 without setting FLB_LIB_OK when the notification loop finds no started or failed value (:1008). ctx is handed to the engine thread until that thread is joined; v1 cannot state a thread start, a join or that retention. Whether a start after flb_stop can run again (flb_stop destroys ctx->config->cf_main, :1066-1069, and the engine thread's run is not read) is not derived, so the lifecycle permits a start only from created.", "qcore-c integrator", rewrite_before_acceptance);
    ensures normal resource_borrowed(ctx);
    ensures normal resource_return_none();
};

extern fn flb_stop(ctx: ptr<mut, u8>) -> i32
contract {
    requires resource_contract_v1;
    requires resource_lifecycle(ctx, stop);
    requires resource_review_pending("Observed at 90978ac6 (src/flb_lib.c), checked by the rule 10q census: flb_stop reads ctx->config->worker (:1047) before its !ctx->config test (:1062), and ctx->config is stored only by flb_create before publication (:174), so the return 0 without a join at :1063 is derived infeasible under P-DEREF. On the FLB_LIB_NONE or FLB_LIB_ERROR status branch (:1049) it joins the engine thread and returns 0 with the join result ignored (:1058). Otherwise it signals the engine to exit and returns the join result (:1077-1083). Every feasible path joins ctx->config->worker and nothing clears it, so the lifecycle permits a stop only after a flb_start that returned 0, once; a stop after a failed start, a second stop or a stop of a never-started context is a caller violation, whose second join or join of an unwritten identifier the native observation reproduced (W47-FB-DOUBLE-STOP-OBSERVATION). The status read (:1049) is not synchronised with the engine thread's own write (:932).", "qcore-c integrator");
    ensures normal resource_borrowed(ctx);
    ensures normal resource_return_none();
};

extern fn flb_destroy(ctx: ptr<mut, u8>) -> unit
contract {
    requires resource_contract_v1;
    requires resource_lifecycle(ctx, destroy);
    requires resource_review_pending("Observed at 90978ac6 (src/flb_lib.c:230-263), identical at 046accf6 and 66910c10, read by the rule 10q census: flb_destroy returns at once on a null ctx; otherwise it deletes and frees ctx->event_channel, destroys ctx->event_loop, shuts the engine down when ctx->config->is_running is still true (flb_engine_shutdown) and releases ctx->config (flb_config_exit), then frees ctx. Its own body contains no join and never joins ctx->config->worker; the joins its callees reach are the output and input workers' and, through the chunk-trace pipeline (src/flb_chunk_trace.c:106, :270), another context's flb_stop, which the entry-keyed census cannot tell from this context's engine thread (P-JOIN-IDENTITY). So a destroy while this context's engine thread is live frees what that thread retains: the lifecycle clause L6 (destroy only from created, after a failed start, or after the stop) is a precondition on the caller, not an effect of the body; its admission after a failed start rests on do_start having joined the thread it spawned (L7, checked against flb_start's body). Whether the nullable release of ctx is the whole custody effect (the members it frees, the engine shutdown's own releases) is not derived here.", "qcore-c integrator");
    ensures normal resource_released(ctx, nullable);
    ensures normal resource_return_none();
};

extern fn flb_input_thread_instance_exit(ins: ptr<mut, u8>) -> i32
contract {
    requires resource_contract_v1;
    requires resource_review_pending("Read at 90978ac6 (src/flb_input_thread.c), not derived by the census: it copies the input worker's identifier (:591), writes the exit command to the worker and joins it with the result ignored (:607), returning 0. When the command cannot be written it returns -1 without joining (:602-604); whether a caller then releases what the worker retains is not derived.", "qcore-c integrator");
    ensures normal resource_borrowed(ins);
    ensures normal resource_return_none();
};

extern fn flb_output_thread_pool_destroy(ins: ptr<mut, u8>) -> unit
contract {
    requires resource_contract_v1;
    requires resource_review_pending("Read at 90978ac6 (src/flb_output_thread.c), not derived by the census: for each running pool worker it writes the stop command and joins the worker with the result ignored (:646), then destroys the pool (:650). Candidate, not a verified defect: a worker whose stop command cannot be written is skipped without a join (:640-644) and its parameters are freed while it may still run; confirming it needs a native reproduction of the failed write.", "qcore-c integrator");
    ensures normal resource_borrowed(ins);
    ensures normal resource_return_none();
};

extern fn flb_downstream_worker_runtime_stop(runtime: ptr<mut, u8>) -> i32
contract {
    requires resource_contract_v1;
    requires resource_review_pending("Read at 90978ac6 (src/flb_downstream_worker.c), not derived by the census: called from one of its own workers it returns -1 without joining (:491-492, which avoids a self join) and without freeing the runtime; otherwise it waits for active operations under the lifecycle mutex, wakes every worker and joins each created worker with the result ignored (:520) before destroying the runtime's synchronisation objects and freeing it. The runtime is freed on that path and not on the -1 return, which v1 cannot state per path.", "qcore-c integrator", rewrite_before_acceptance);
    ensures normal resource_released(runtime, nullable);
    ensures normal resource_return_none();
};
